ccbench-remote patch 0007 -- give every scheme binary its own output file
=========================================================================

Applied by docker/apply-patches.py :: wrapper_stdout_capture().
Creates src/helpers/ccb_log.py and redirects stdout+stderr of the scheme
binaries in src/wrappers/{pcc,vivace,taova,indigo}.py.

WHAT IS WRONG UPSTREAM
----------------------
src/experiments/tunnel_manager.py:64 spawns the tunnel process with

    procs[tun_id] = Popen(cmd_to_run, stdin=PIPE, stdout=PIPE, preexec_fn=os.setsid)

and the scheme binary is started *inside* that process, so it inherits the same
pipe. test.py reads the pipe only for the connection handshake and then sleeps
out the runtime; nothing drains it again. Everything the binary prints collects
in the 64 KiB kernel pipe buffer, and once that is full the binary BLOCKS in
write(2) and never returns.

PCC-Allegro walks into this for two reasons at once:
  * third_party/pcc/sender/app/appclient.cpp:143-167 -- the monitor thread
    prints one stats line per second for the whole run.
  * third_party/pcc/sender/src/core.cpp has LIVE cout calls on the sending
    path: :2487 "DROP!", :2619 "Hurry!"<<..., :2946 "insert". So the thread
    that transmits packets is itself a writer to that pipe.

MEASURED
--------
Two 3600 s uplink runs of pcc over the Tailscale path, pep-star01 -> pep-star02:

    2026-11-04   data departures stop at t=2106 s
    2026-11-05   data departures stop at t=2346.85 s
                 614799 packets / 626846000 B sent, then nothing at all
                 ACKs still arriving until t=2399.53 s
                 largest gap anywhere before that point: < 2 s
                 no error in test-uplink-pcc.log, "Done testing pcc", RC=0
                 log spans 65% of runtime -> discarded by src/analysis/plot.py
                 (`duration < 0.8 * self.runtime`), giving `{"pcc": {}}`

65536 B of pipe divided by the ~28 B of a typical monitor line is ~2340 lines
at one line per second, which is where the 2026-11-05 run stopped. The two runs
differ because the trigger is cumulative BYTES printed, and that depends on how
many digits are in the numbers being printed.

WHAT THIS IS NOT
----------------
Not a congestion-control event, not loss, not a path change: the path sampler
recorded distinct_routes=1 for the whole hour and there is no stall before the
freeze. It is the same class of defect as patches/0004 (taova onduration =
1000 s) and patches/0006 (iperf -t 75): a hard-coded assumption that nobody
runs the benchmark this long.

THE EDIT
--------
New file src/helpers/ccb_log.py exposing open_log(scheme, role), which returns
an unbuffered append handle on

    ${CCB_WRAPPER_LOG_DIR:-/pantheon/tmp}/ccb-wrapper-<scheme>-<role>-<pid>.log

Unbuffered so a binary killed by test.py at the end of the run still leaves
behind everything it printed.

Then, in each of the four wrappers, both roles:

-        check_call(cmd)
+        with ccb_log.open_log('<scheme>', '<role>') as handle:
+            check_call(cmd, stdout=handle, stderr=handle)

and in pcc.py specifically:

-        # suppress debugging output to stderr
-        with open(os.devnull, 'w') as devnull:
-            check_call(cmd, stderr=devnull)
+        with ccb_log.open_log('pcc', 'sender') as handle:
+            check_call(cmd, stdout=handle, stderr=handle)

WHY ALL FOUR WRAPPERS AND NOT JUST PCC
--------------------------------------
All four inherit the same pipe. taova, indigo and vivace completing an hour is
luck about how much they print, not safety -- vivace is the same UDT codebase as
pcc, shipped pre-compiled, so its print volume cannot even be inspected. Fixing
only pcc would leave the other three one verbose build away from the same
silent truncation.

Discarding pcc's stderr was also the single reason the failure had no witness,
so that stops too.

EFFECT ON THE MEASUREMENT
-------------------------
None on the algorithms: no scheme binary is recompiled and no protocol
parameter changes. It moves where their console output lands. The write(2) that
used to block on a full pipe now lands in a file, which is both the fix and the
evidence.

scripts/11_run_matrix.sh :: collect_wrapper_logs() copies both sides into the
cell directory as wrapper-local.log / wrapper-remote.log after every cell.
